# Fixed 2026-09-30, defect 130's class, lane recovery-b1 (lane X4's finding
# n4), beside `fixedbugs-130-a-literal-the-lexer-refused-is-still-a-value`,
# whose statement-level half this finishes. Symptom: inside brackets a
# literal the lexer refused still ended its expression, so `f(0X10 + )` and
# `[0X20, )]` cost the literal's spelling alone, and `check --apply`,
# writing `0x10` and `0x20`, wrote a program refused for the `)` each
# holds, a mistake no run had told; so did a digit not in its base, a
# misplaced `_`, a missing `,` in a call's arguments or a map's entries, a
# condition's `)`, and a character literal. Cause: the expression reader
# built an `error` node at a refused token, which ends an expression, and
# only a statement's end read past one. Now a refused literal whose end the
# lexer found is read as the literal it stands for, at any depth
# (`selfhost/parse/refused_literal.hero`); one that never closed, `"abc +
# )`, took its line's closers with it and is left as it was.

function main()
    a = f(0X10 + )  #~ base_prefix_case expected_expression
    b = [0X20, )]  #~ base_prefix_case expected_expression
    c = (0X30 + )  #~ base_prefix_case expected_expression
    d = g(0X40, 2 3)  #~ base_prefix_case expected_args_close
    e = {0X5: 2 3}  #~ base_prefix_case expected_separator
    h = [1, 0b102, )]  #~ digit_not_in_base expected_expression
    i = f(1_000_ + )  #~ misplaced_separator expected_expression
    j = f('ab' + )  #~ char_literal expected_expression
    if 0X60 > 1 )  #~ base_prefix_case expected_end_of_line
        print(1)
    k = f(0X70)  #~ base_prefix_case
    print(a, b, c, d, e, h, i, j, k)

function f(n: i64) -> i64
    return n

function g(a: i64, b: i64) -> i64
    return a
